    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.effective_size - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.effective_size.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.effective_size()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate Burt's effective size of every node's network.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.effective_size()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Calculate Burt's effective size of every node's network.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.effective_size()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Calculate Burt's effective size of every node's network.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCalculate Burt's effective size of every node's network.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L63211-L63320){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.effective_size(\n    weighted=None,\n    attr_name=None,\n    return_dict=False,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Effective size](../assets/graphs/effective-size.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The shared seven-node strip of triangles with a tail, shaded by Burt's effective size: the number of contacts minus how redundant they are with one another. Hub C has four contacts that partly know each other and scores 2.50, the bridge E 2.33, and F, whose two contacts E and G are unlinked, a full 2.00; A and G score 1.00.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nEffective size (Burt, 1992) is the number of a node's contacts, discounted for\nredundancy: contacts who are tied to one another bring overlapping information and\ncount for less. A node whose contacts all know each other has an effective size close to\n1; a broker whose contacts are strangers to one another has an effective size equal to\nits degree. It is the classic measure of *structural holes*, the gaps in a network that\na well-placed broker can bridge. For the complementary measure of how constrained a node\nis, see `constraint()`.\n\nFor an unweighted undirected graph this is `degree - 2t / degree`, where `t` is the\nnumber of ties among the node's contacts. Weighted and directed graphs use Burt's\ngeneral formula, with tie strengths as proportions of each node's total. Self-loops are\nignored.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to use edge weights as tie strengths. If `None` (default), follows the graph.\n\n<code>[attr_name]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the node attribute to store scores in. If `None` (default), uses\n    `\"effective_size\"`.\n\n<code>[return_dict]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, returns a dictionary instead of self. If `False` (default), stores the\n    scores as a node attribute and returns self for chaining.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Union[dict[int, float | None], Graph]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `return_dict=` is `True`: a dictionary mapping node IDs to effective sizes\n    (`None` for nodes without contacts). If `return_dict=` is `False` (default): returns\n    self.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~constraint`) \n\n:   The other half of Burt's structural holes measures.\n\n[](`~local_constraint`) \n\n:   The constraint that one neighbor puts on a node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe broker B links three contacts who don't know one another and keeps its full effective\nsize of `3`, while C, whose two contacts know each other, has only `1`:\n\n::: {#2303690a .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"B\", \"A\"), (\"B\", \"X\"), (\"B\", \"Y\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"E\"),\n     (\"D\", \"E\")]\n)\n\ngraph.effective_size().get_node_df().select(\n    \"label\", \"degree\", \"effective_size\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (7, 3)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>degree</th><th>effective_size</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;B&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>3.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;A&quot;</td><td>1</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;X&quot;</td><td>1</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Y&quot;</td><td>1</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;C&quot;</td><td>2</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;D&quot;</td><td>2</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;E&quot;</td><td>2</td><td>1.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Redundant in-laws\n\nEffective size is a node's number of contacts, less the redundancy among them. In the\nmarriage network of the Florentine families, the Guadagni keep all four of their ties,\nwhile the Strozzi count for three, and the Peruzzi for under two: the Strozzi, one of\ntheir three in-laws, married the other two:\n\n::: {#bea127db .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\nflorentine = ec.florentine_families().effective_size()\n\n(\n    florentine.get_node_df()\n    .select(\"label\", \"degree\", \"effective_size\")\n    .with_columns(redundant=pl.col(\"degree\") - pl.col(\"effective_size\"))\n    .filter(pl.col(\"degree\") >= 3)\n    .sort(\"redundant\", \"label\")\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (9, 4)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>label</th><th>degree</th><th>effective_size</th><th>redundant</th></tr><tr><td>str</td><td>i64</td><td>f64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;Albizzi&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>3.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Guadagni&quot;</td><td>4</td><td>4.0</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Medici&quot;</td><td>6</td><td>5.666667</td><td>0.333333</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Bischeri&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>2.333333</td><td>0.666667</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Castellani&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>2.333333</td><td>0.666667</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Ridolfi&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>2.333333</td><td>0.666667</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Tornabuoni&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>2.333333</td><td>0.666667</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Strozzi&quot;</td><td>4</td><td>3.0</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Peruzzi&quot;</td><td>3</td><td>1.666667</td><td>1.333333</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nSized and colored by effective size, the Medici stand out as the family with the most\nnon-redundant contacts:\n\n::: {#ac7883b8 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nflorentine.plot(\n    size_by=\"effective_size\", color_by=\"effective_size\", label_by=\"label\",\n    seed=5,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-66b03f8b:#618fc7;--ect-88fdb3f7:#6b9ad2;--ect-692e2bbf:#507db4;--ect-4eae359b:#74a3dc;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-850c075a:#376299;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-6970a796:#5d7fa7;--ect-f241547a:#43648b;--ect-c0015a99:#4a6b93;--ect-5aa54a62:#38577d;--ect-875b57dc:#51729a;--ect-bf50105f:#09254a;--ect-f8f129c2:#26446b;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-975840b8:#184f95;--ect-66b03f8b:#4d79b3;--ect-88fdb3f7:#3f6dab;--ect-692e2bbf:#668dc1;--ect-4eae359b:#3363a4;--ect-6425850a:#cde2fb;--ect-850c075a:#8bacd7;--ect-f8094ac6:#2c5d9f;--ect-472663e1:#3d6caa;--ect-6826a5f9:#4f7ab4;--ect-9d43aea2:#6089be;--ect-5ee57d66:#7297c9;--ect-3c980f0e:#84a6d3;--ect-ec386ef0:#96b5dd;--ect-97f76ec5:#a8c4e7;--ect-e28c6970:#bad3f1;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-6970a796:#103768;--ect-f241547a:#35547d;--ect-c0015a99:#2c4c77;--ect-5aa54a62:#476287;--ect-875b57dc:#234572;--ect-bf501